+
+**Archive sufficiency and the governing power statement**
+
+**Verified.** V1.1 changes the design to 16 learner seeds, two worlds per date and approximately 160 claim dates. It explicitly **withdraws and replaces** v1 §E's analytic power statement with the measured synthetic calibration table: at 16 seeds, conjunction success 0.675 ± 0.065 at 5% date SD, and 0.980 ± 0.019 at 3% date SD, with seed SD 1% and planning alternative +2% against the +0.5% margin. It discloses nominal-95% interval undercoverage. V1.2 preserves the panel and margins and additionally requires calibration of the decision bound's own one-sided coverage. That is the governing statement before this allocation amendment, not the original “+1% is undetectable” paragraph. [H/V025-STAGES-6-8-CONTRACT-v1.1-AMENDMENT-2026-09-09.md:3–17; H/V025-STAGES-6-8-CONTRACT-v1.2-AMENDMENT-2026-09-09.md:3,8.]
+
+**Verified.** The requested empirical SD is **0.751 percentage points** across 30 dates for `ORACLE_SET / UNILATERAL − 1`, after each date pools bits and energy over its anchors. The aggregate records `sample_sd = 0.007512355732348572`; this is a relative-gain SD, not the contract's simulated 5% log-effect SD. The report is descriptive over fixed TRAIN dates and contains no learner. Its 196 unequal anchors also differ from the contracted two-world learned panel. [M/CEILING30-MARGIN-2026-09-10.md:5,29–35,122,140–142; M/.scratch/oracle-ceiling30-margin/ceiling30-margin-aggregate.json:116–121.]
+
+**Derived, paper planning only.** To avoid silently changing scales, use percentage-point relative gains here: s = 0.751, practical margin δ = 0.5. For independent date contributions with negligible other variance and a Gaussian known-SD approximation,
+
+`D ≈ ceil{[(z_0.975 + z_power) s / (Δ* − δ)]²}`.
+
+The contract's actual prospective alternative is **+2%**, not the observed +0.717% oracle mean. The +1% and +0.75% rows below are fixed sensitivity scenarios. For m simultaneous required inequalities, setting each marginal failure probability to at most `0.20/m` gives at least 80% conjunction power by the union bound, without assuming independent contrasts. This is a power calculation, not a Bonferroni change to the sealed intersection–union test.
+
+| Prospective effect versus +0.5% margin | One contrast, 80% | Three-contrast conjunction, ≥80% | Six-inequality conjunction, ≥80% |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| +2% (contract alternative) | 2 | 4 | 4 |
+| +1% sensitivity | 18 | 28 | 33 |
+| +0.75% sensitivity | 71 | 109 | 130 |
+
+These are **date-only normal-approximation counts**, not valid tiny-sample bootstrap designs. In particular, “2 dates” is not a recommendation to perform inference with two clusters. Keeping 48 supplies replication and temporal coverage well above those optimistic +2% counts. For one +1% contrast it also accommodates approximately twice the nominal date variance (36 dates by the same arithmetic). At D=48, date-only SE is 0.1084 percentage points and the Gaussian 95% half-width is 0.2125 points. All inputs and calculations are stated; none uses a route's observed sign.
+
+**Derived limitation.** For a learned contrast, even granting the oracle SD as a planning proxy, `Var ≈ s_date²/D + s_seed²/16 + residual/world terms`. At the old *assumed*, not measured, seed SD of 1 percentage point, the seed-only SE floor is 0.25 points. No number of dates then reaches 80% marginal power for +1% versus +0.5% under this approximation; the requisite total SE is about 0.1785 points. For +2%, the same arithmetic needs approximately 3/5/7 dates for one/three/six contrasts, before residual terms. Thus neither “166 dates cannot work” nor “48 guarantees the learned claim” follows from the receipts. More dates cannot compensate for every variance component.
+
+**Verified → Decision.** A later recorded owner requirement names six strict inequalities, including standalone route comparisons, while saying it changes no sealed threshold. It does not supply a new six-contrast calibration or a minimal positive effect. Arbitrarily small strict positivity has no finite universal sample-size requirement. The six-column sensitivity above does not reinterpret that requirement as permission to lower the +0.5% sealed margin. [C/V025-CONTROLLER-REQUIREMENT-SHARPENED-2026-09-10.md:3–5,12–27.]
+
+**UNDETERMINED — evaluation dates required for the actual learned conjunction.** A single matched development-panel study of the frozen learned FULL/DROP and required standalone contrasts, crossed over dates and 16 learner seeds with additive bits/energy/QoS receipts, would supply the missing variance/covariance inputs; its predeclared analysis must calibrate the actual pooled decision bound and conjunction at the fixed planning alternative and allocation. Oracle SD alone cannot answer it. This is the remaining statistical measurement, not a reopening of the source/evaluation allocation. No claim of calibrated power transfers from the withdrawn analytic paragraph, from the 160-date table, or from this 48-date arithmetic.
+
+**Decision on sufficiency.** The archive supplies the **48** allocated evaluation dates and the **116** source/development dates. It is sufficient for this operational allocation and, conditionally, for date-only planning at the contract's +2% alternative and the +1% sensitivity. It is insufficient for the +0.75% date-only scenario and for a 160-fresh-date claim panel after retaining this source allocation. The final learned claim's power is UNDETERMINED, rather than certified by substituting a convenient SD. The outcome rule may return inconclusive; no positive result is owed by the allocation.
+
+**World generation: backlog, cost and shortfall**
+
+**Verified → Inferred.** D=1 describes the completed learner evaluation on `V025_PROBE/world/3`, 2025-11-16. It is a world-generation/evaluation backlog in a restricted pilot, not the intended scientific design: the minimal pilot explicitly used one evaluation world and two learner seeds; the controller finding names new-world generation as the repair, while the contract calls for approximately 160 dates. Training sources used worlds 1/2 on two dates; repeated source epochs and checkpoints create neither new source dates nor new evaluation dates. [C/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-ONE-DATE-2026-09-09.md:11–18,27–33; /home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/V025-PILOT-MIN-REPORT-2026-09-09.md:7–24; W/DATE-VARIANCE-2026-09-09.md:65–72; H/V025-STAGES-6-8-CONTRACT-v1-2026-09-08.md:28.]
+
+**Verified cost, qualified.** FINDING-CEILING-INTERVAL:29 reports about eight wall minutes and 0.3 core-hours per date at eight to ten anchors. Actual aggregate receipt entries corroborate this order of magnitude: a ten-anchor date used 0.3261 core-hours and 508.67 seconds; the next eight-anchor date used 0.3175 core-hours and 493.88 seconds. Across the complete, mostly six-anchor panel, the exact totals are 6.1677 core-hours and 9,975.81 wall seconds for 30 dates, including 1,131.27 seconds of construction. Therefore 0.3 is a reasonable denser-ladder allowance, **not** the measured all-panel mean, which is 0.2056 core-hours and 5.542 wall minutes/date. [C/V025-CONTROLLER-FINDING-CEILING-INTERVAL-2026-09-10.md:28–29; M/.scratch/oracle-ceiling30/ceiling30-aggregate.json:113–139,545–549; A/CEILING30-CODEX-2026-09-10.md:72,76,79–82.]
+
+**Derived planning, four total processes maximum, no launch here.** At 0.3 core-hours per date, ideal wall time is `0.3 × date_count / 4` hours. That assumes four fully useful cores; actual ladder receipts used one coordinator plus three selection workers and achieve less than ideal utilisation. Budget the approximately eight-minute observed denser-date wall rate as a second, more conservative projection:
+
+| Work remaining or counterfactual | Date count | Core-hours allowance | Ideal wall at 4 processes | At ~8 wall minutes/date |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| New evaluation assignments, beyond two retained reservations | 46 | 13.8 | 3.45 h | 6.13 h |
+| Complete fresh claim-date ladder, crediting no completed claim outcome | 48 | 14.4 | 3.60 h | 6.40 h |
+| Newly available source dates, if all receive the same ladder | 70 | 21.0 | 5.25 h | 9.33 h |
+| Extra dates to restore 160 evaluations while retaining S and Q | 112 | 33.6 | 8.40 h | 14.93 h |
+
+The one completed learner date and the 30 opened diagnostic dates contribute **zero completed fresh claim dates**. Unopened reservations establish assignment, not completed claim evaluation. Tape existence also cannot be inferred from the ceiling receipts: their tapes were held in RAM and not serialised. [W/DATE-VARIANCE-2026-09-09.md:69–80; A/CEILING30-CODEX-2026-09-10.md:82.]
+
+**Decision.** No new archive dates are required to implement the adopted allocation. More worlds on its dates are required. World generation can now be scheduled against S and E without another allocation decision; all independent physics/admission and integrity gates continue to apply. This adjudication launches nothing. The ladder allowance includes search/evaluation and is not a measured cost for full source labelling, 16-seed training, or two-world-per-date full-horizon claim evaluation. Their total wall time is **UNDETERMINED**; one representative end-to-end contracted date receipt at a fixed four-process cap would settle the per-date cost. Generating worlds cannot manufacture the 112 missing calendar dates in the rejected 160-date layout: that would first require a separately frozen archive expansion, never TEST reuse.
+
+**Strongest case against this allocation, and answer**
+
+The strongest objection is that 48 is an engineering envelope supported by variance from the wrong experiment. It reduces a sealed 160-date panel; the measured oracle SD need not match learned FULL-versus-DROP pooled influence variance; neighbouring dates can remain dependent; and holding out dates does not guarantee useful learning from the source pool. Even the date-only +0.75% sensitivity needs 71 dates. A conditional familiar-date evaluation with independently generated worlds could validly overlap source dates and preserve more evaluation coverage. The two historically conflicting reservations and incomplete global lineage further weaken any blanket claim of freshness. These objections follow from the estimand distinction, arithmetic and enforcement findings above, not from whether a preferred route won.
+
+**Answer and final ruling.** I accept those limitations and reject the claim that a 160-date calibration already certifies this experiment. I nevertheless choose **116 source/development, 48 evaluation-only, two quarantined**, because it fixes the missing operational boundary, preserves broad training support and clean start-date evaluation, and is compatible with measured date-only precision at the pre-existing +2% planning alternative without consuming TEST or choosing favourable outcomes. The panel estimates performance on these source-excluded historical TRAIN starts; it is not asserted to be a probability sample of all 166 dates. Actual claim power and residual date dependence must be reported honestly, and failure remains an admissible outcome. The allocation is final; uncertainty about efficacy is not authority to change its dates, margins, model or sign rules after results.
+
+Verification performed: read-only documents, source and existing JSON receipts; one `nice -n 15 /usr/bin/python3 -B` process using standard-library calendar/set arithmetic and normal-quantile planning from literal recorded inputs. No physical measurement, simulation, training, TLE-content read, dependency install, sealed-file mutation, or modification of `/home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover` or its `.venv`. This decision file is the sole new deliverable.

